1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
ReadyRoute: A clear plan before. A clearer picture during.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:11,250
Emergencies create two problems at once. Households need a plan they can find quickly, and

3
00:00:11,250 --> 00:00:16,750
responders need requests they can compare. People who face transport, language, or power

4
00:00:16,750 --> 00:00:23,250
barriers may be especially hard to reach. ReadyRoute is our working, offline prototype for those gaps.

5
00:00:23,250 --> 00:00:29,250
It has three views. First, a household saves an out-of-area contact, meeting place, backup

6
00:00:29,250 --> 00:00:36,000
route, and any access needs. Second, someone can make a short report with a location, need

7
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:43,250
type, urgency, and barriers. Third, a coordinator sees an explained queue and can mark a request assigned.

8
00:00:43,250 --> 00:00:49,250
Here is the household flow. In My plan, I enter sample details for the Rivera household: Elena

9
00:00:49,250 --> 00:00:55,750
as a contact, North library as a meeting point, and an accessible van as a backup. I save it

10
00:00:55,750 --> 00:01:03,000
locally and can print a copy. There is no account. The interface also has larger text and a partial Spanish option.

11
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:08,250
Now consider the response view. A fictional request for medical support at Pine Avenue

12
00:01:08,250 --> 00:01:13,500
apartments says help is needed right now and power-dependent equipment is involved. The queue

13
00:01:13,500 --> 00:01:19,250
shows thirty points for urgency, ten for an essential need, and six for an access barrier.

14
00:01:19,250 --> 00:01:24,750
ReadyRoute suggests a medical backup-power team because it matches the need and has capacity. A

15
00:01:24,750 --> 00:01:30,250
coordinator chooses whether to assign it, which reduces the available count. A water request at

16
00:01:30,250 --> 00:01:35,000
North library remains visible below. The app never dispatches help on its own.

17
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:41,250
The concept could make intake faster, explain prioritization more clearly, and surface access

18
00:01:41,250 --> 00:01:48,000
barriers. We have not measured real-world impact. Before any real pilot, ReadyRoute would need

19
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:52,500
official data, secure synchronization, consent, community testing, and emergency-management

20
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:58,000
partners. This demonstration is not an official warning or dispatch system.
